    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Steinauer, Nebraska, was founded in 1856 by three Swiss brothers—Joseph, Anton, and Nicholas—who fled famine and depression in their homeland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steinauer%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in 1874, cementing the village’s name and serving as its first official federal presence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steinauer%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Chicago, Rock Island, and Pacific Railroad arrived in 1887, prompting a formal platting that aligned the town’s grid with the tracks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steinauer%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Steinauer was officially incorporated as a village in March 1893, establishing a board of trustees under Nebraska law.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
